Close the kqchan fd even when EPOLL_CTL_DEL fails, or a dead channel spins forever.

When ciderd exits, a guest that outlives it burns a whole core in epoll_pwait and never dies,
holding a /proc gc-root open on its store path: the real teardown hang (#15). strace of the survivor
is two syscalls turning as fast as the loop allows -- gettid then epoll_pwait -- returning the same
descriptor EPOLLIN|EPOLLHUP every time, with no recv between them. That is a level-triggered dead fd
that nothing ever removes from the epoll set.

The descriptor is a kqchan socket to the daemon. evfilt_machport_knote_delete and
evfilt_proc_knote_delete drop it from the kqueue's epoll set with EPOLL_CTL_DEL and then close it, but
on a FAILED del they returned -1 before reaching the close. knote_delete ignores that return and frees
the knote regardless, so the socket stayed both open and still registered, its epoll entry now
pointing at freed memory. linux_kevent_copyout reads that freed knote, sees filter 0, discards the
event as a zero-filter wakeup, and the caller is handed nothing and wakes on the same ready fd again
at once. No recv is ever issued because no filter copyout runs. That is the whole spin.

The close is what actually removes the fd from the epoll set, so it must run whether or not the DEL
succeeded. Make the DEL best-effort and fall through to the close in both kqchan-backed filters.
---
--- a/src/linux/machport.c
+++ b/src/linux/machport.c
@@ -272,10 +272,17 @@
 evfilt_machport_knote_delete(struct filter *filt, struct knote *kn)
 {
     if ((kn->kev.flags & EV_DISABLE) == 0) {
-        if (epoll_ctl(kn->kn_epollfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, kn->kdata.kn_dupfd, NULL) < 0) {
+        /*
+         * Best-effort, and DO NOT bail before the close below. The old code returned here on a
+         * failed EPOLL_CTL_DEL, leaving kn_dupfd both open AND still registered in the epoll set;
+         * knote_delete ignores this return and frees the knote regardless, so epoll then reports
+         * that dead fd forever (level-triggered EPOLLHUP once the daemon exits), linux_kevent_copyout
+         * sees a zero-filter knote and discards it, and the caller spins on epoll_wait at 100% CPU --
+         * the ciderd-teardown busy-loop (#15). The close is what actually removes the fd from epoll,
+         * so it must always run.
+         */
+        if (epoll_ctl(kn->kn_epollfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, kn->kdata.kn_dupfd, NULL) < 0)
             dbg_perror("epoll_ctl(2)");
-            return (-1);
-        }
     }

 	(void) __close_for_kqueue(kn->kdata.kn_dupfd);
--- a/src/linux/proc.c
+++ b/src/linux/proc.c
@@ -254,10 +254,15 @@
 evfilt_proc_knote_delete(struct filter *filt, struct knote *kn)
 {
     if ((kn->kev.flags & EV_DISABLE) == 0) {
-        if (epoll_ctl(kn->kn_epollfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, kn->kdata.kn_dupfd, NULL) < 0) {
+        /*
+         * Best-effort, and DO NOT bail before the close below -- same bug as
+         * evfilt_machport_knote_delete: returning here on a failed EPOLL_CTL_DEL leaves kn_dupfd
+         * open and still in the epoll set while knote_delete frees the knote, so epoll reports the
+         * dead fd forever and the caller spins (#15, ciderd teardown). The close removes the fd
+         * from epoll, so it must always run.
+         */
+        if (epoll_ctl(kn->kn_epollfd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, kn->kdata.kn_dupfd, NULL) < 0)
             dbg_perror("epoll_ctl(2)");
-            return (-1);
-        }
     }

 	(void) __close_for_kqueue(kn->kdata.kn_dupfd);
